.TH PY-FOLIATION 1 "" "py-foliation 0.1" "py-foliation"
.SH NAME
py-foliation \- change the page labels of PDF documents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
py-foliation [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [--version] COMMAND ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Change the page labels of a PDF: the numbering a PDF viewer shows for its
pages (i, ii, iii ... 1, 2, 3 ... A-1, A-2 ...).

positional arguments:
  COMMAND
    show               show the page labels of PDFs
    set                give PDFs new page labels
    clear              remove the page labels of PDFs
    copy               give PDFs the page labels of another
    completion         print the completion script of a shell
    interface          show or choose the language of the interface

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  --version            show program's version number and exit

Run 'py-foliation COMMAND -h' for help on a command. 'py-foliation file.pdf'
on its own shows the page labels of the file.
.fi
.SH COMMANDS
.SS show
.nf
usage: py-foliation show [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [-a | -l LABEL]
                         [--password PASSWORD]
                         FILE [FILE ...]

Show the ranges of page labels of PDFs, and the command that would give them
to another file.

positional arguments:
  FILE                 source PDF

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -a, --all            show the label of every page
  -l, --label LABEL    show the pages labelled LABEL (if none, those
                       labelled so but for capitals)
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                       kept when they are saved)
.fi
.SS set
.nf
usage: py-foliation set [-h] [--interface {en,es}]
                        [-r PAGES [KEY=VALUE] [PAGES [KEY=VALUE] ...]]
                        [-f LIST] [-k] [--remove PAGE] [-o OUTPUT | -i] [-n]
                        [--password PASSWORD]
                        FILE|RANGE [FILE|RANGE ...]

Give PDFs new page labels, range by range. The labels they had are replaced, unless --keep is given.

positional arguments:
  FILE|RANGE            the PDFs (files ending in .pdf), and the ranges:
                        PAGES:STYLE[:START[:PREFIX]]

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}   the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                        keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -r, --range PAGES [KEY=VALUE] [PAGES [KEY=VALUE] ...]
                        a range: PAGES [style=S] [start=N] [prefix=P]
                        [suffix=X]
  -f, --from-file LIST  the labels of all the pages, one on each line (- for
                        the standard input)
  -k, --keep            keep the labels the PDF has, changing only the pages
                        given
  --remove PAGE         drop the range that starts at PAGE (the one before
                        goes on over its pages); implies --keep
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output PDF, with one input only (default:
                        <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place        change the input files themselves
  -n, --dry-run         show the labels the files would get, without writing
                        anything
  --password PASSWORD   the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                        kept when they are saved)

A range is written PAGES:STYLE[:START[:PREFIX]]:

  1:r            pages from 1 on: i, ii, iii ...
  9:D            pages from 9 on: 1, 2, 3 ...
  201-214:A      pages 201 to 214: A ... N (then the old numbering goes on)
  215:D:1:App-   pages from 215 on: App-1, App-2 ...
  5:f:12v        folios from page 5 on: 12v, 13r, 13v ...
  LAST:-::Cover  the last page: Cover

or, with --range, PAGES [style=S] [start=N] [prefix=P] [suffix=X]:

  --range 9-200 style=arabic
  --range 1-4 style=roman prefix=[ suffix=]

PAGES is a first page (9) or a span (9-200, 9..200, 201-); LAST and LAST-N
count from the end. A range without a last page goes on until the next one.

Styles: D or arabic (1, 2, 3), r or roman (i, ii, iii), R or ROMAN (I, II,
III), a or letters (a, b, c), A or LETTERS (A, B, C), f or folio (1r, 1v,
2r: the recto and verso of each leaf; START is a leaf and its side, as
12v), - or none (the prefix only).

Folios and suffixes are not page label styles of PDF: py-foliation writes
them as one label for each page, and recognises them when it reads the
file.

--from-file takes the labels of all the pages, one on each line (as
\&'show --all' writes them, with or without the page numbers; - is the
standard input), and finds the fewest ranges that give them.
.fi
.SS clear
.nf
usage: py-foliation clear [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [-o OUTPUT | -i] [-n]
                          [--password PASSWORD]
                          FILE [FILE ...]

Remove the page labels of PDFs: viewers then number their pages 1, 2, 3 ...

positional arguments:
  FILE                 source PDF

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT  output PDF, with one input only (default:
                       <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place       change the input files themselves
  -n, --dry-run        show the labels the files would get, without writing
                       anything
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                       kept when they are saved)
.fi
.SS copy
.nf
usage: py-foliation copy [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [-o OUTPUT | -i] [-n]
                         [--password PASSWORD]
                         FROM FILE [FILE ...]

Give PDFs the page labels of another PDF, for instance a new export of the
same book. If they have fewer pages, the labels of the pages they lack are
left out; if they have more, the last range goes on.

positional arguments:
  FROM                 the PDF whose labels are copied
  FILE                 the PDFs that get them

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  -o, --output OUTPUT  output PDF, with one input only (default:
                       <input>-labelled.pdf)
  -i, --in-place       change the input files themselves
  -n, --dry-run        show the labels the files would get, without writing
                       anything
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDFs (their protection is
                       kept when they are saved)
.fi
.SS completion
.nf
usage: py-foliation completion [-h] [--interface {en,es}] SHELL

Print the script that completes py-foliation's commands, options and files
in SHELL. For instance: py-foliation completion fish >
~/.config/fish/completions/py-foliation.fish

positional arguments:
  SHELL                bash, fish or zsh

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SS interface
.nf
usage: py-foliation interface [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [{en,es}]

The language of the help, the messages and the window. The choice is kept in
the configuration file; --interface CODE changes it for one run only.

positional arguments:
  {en,es}              the language to use from now on; without it, the
                       languages are listed and the one in use is marked

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SH FILES
The configuration file, with the language of the interface: ~/.config/py-foliation/config.toml (or $PY_FOLIATION_CONFIG/config.toml).
.SH SEE ALSO
The window: py-foliation-gui [file.pdf].
.SH AUTHOR
Nicolas Vaughan <https://nicolasvaughan.org>
